   "id": "m02s4",
   "title": "The NATO Air Tasking Cycle",
   "html": "<blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>4.15 The ATO articulates tasking for air operations for a specific time, normally 24 hours. Detailed planning takes place within a predetermined and recurring schedule. Because of the finite period covered by an ATO, there are typically several ATOs in various stages of development at any one time. Long-endurance missions should be reflected in sequential ATOs. The air tasking cycle starts with the joint commander’s guidance and ends after the assessment period. Long-range combat air assets or long endurance ISR assets launching from outside the JOA, may be airborne before ATO execution. These assets require the most current ATO (and ACO) information and updates available. The targeting cycle matches dedicated targets with the capabilities or forces made available to the CFACC for the given ATO. The number of air tasking cycle phases may vary based on theatre and contingency requirements, but usually consists of the following phases.</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 44</cite></blockquote><blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>Phase 1 – Strategy &amp; plan development Phase 2 – Scheme of manoeuvre &amp; target development 45 Phase 3 – Weaponeering and air allocation 45</p><p>Phase 4 - Air tasking order development 46</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 45</cite></blockquote><blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>Phase 2 – Scheme of manoeuvre &amp; target development 45 Phase 3 – Weaponeering and air allocation 45</p><p>Phase 4 - Air tasking order development 46</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 45</cite></blockquote><blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>Phase 3 – Weaponeering and air allocation 45</p><p>Phase 4 - Air tasking order development 46</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 45</cite></blockquote><blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>Phase 4 - Air tasking order development 46</p><p>Phase 5 – Execution 46 47 Phase 6 – Assessment Section 5 - Guidance, apportionment and targeting 47 Guidance 47 Apportionment 47 Targeting 47 Section 6 - Air and missile defence planning 49 considerations Section 7 - Special operations forces air operations 50 considerations Section 8 - Communication and information systems 50 considerations Section 9 - Electromagnetic operations considerations 50 Signals intelligence considerations 51 Section 10 - Joint intelligence, surveillance and 51 reconnaissance considerations Section 11 - Unmanned aircraft systems planning 52 considerations Section 12 - Force protection considerations 53 Section 13 - Legal considerations for conducting air 54 operations</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 46</cite></blockquote><blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>Phase 5 – Execution 46 47 Phase 6 – Assessment Section 5 - Guidance, apportionment and targeting 47 Guidance 47 Apportionment 47 Targeting 47 Section 6 - Air and missile defence planning 49 considerations Section 7 - Special operations forces air operations 50 considerations Section 8 - Communication and information systems 50 considerations Section 9 - Electromagnetic operations considerations 50 Signals intelligence considerations 51 Section 10 - Joint intelligence, surveillance and 51 reconnaissance considerations Section 11 - Unmanned aircraft systems planning 52 considerations Section 12 - Force protection considerations 53 Section 13 - Legal considerations for conducting air 54 operations Chapter 5 - The space domain 57 Section 1 - Introduction 57 Section 2 - Fundamentals 57 Section 3 - Space domain attributes 59 Physical attributes of the space environment 59 Additional attributes 60 Section 4 - Space domain limitations 60 Section 5 - Space domain operational functions 61 Section 6 - Space domain functional areas 62 Section 7 - Space operations 62</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 46</cite></blockquote><blockquote class=\"quote\"><p>Phase 6 – Assessment Section 5 - Guidance, apportionment and targeting 47 Guidance 47 Apportionment 47 Targeting 47 Section 6 - Air and missile defence planning 49 considerations Section 7 - Special operations forces air operations 50 considerations Section 8 - Communication and information systems 50 considerations Section 9 - Electromagnetic operations considerations 50 Signals intelligence considerations 51 Section 10 - Joint intelligence, surveillance and 51 reconnaissance considerations Section 11 - Unmanned aircraft systems planning 52 considerations Section 12 - Force protection considerations 53 Section 13 - Legal considerations for conducting air 54 operations Chapter 5 - The space domain 57 Section 1 - Introduction 57 Section 2 - Fundamentals 57 Section 3 - Space domain attributes 59 Physical attributes of the space environment 59 Additional attributes 60 Section 4 - Space domain limitations 60 Section 5 - Space domain operational functions 61 Section 6 - Space domain functional areas 62 Section 7 - Space operations 62 Section 8 - Strategic communications and legal 63 considerations for conducting space operations Section 9 - Space domain coordination 64 Overview 64 Space support coordination element considerations 65 Implementing space support coordination elements at the 66 strategic/operational level Implementing space support coordination elements at the 67 tactical level</p><cite>AJP-3.3, p. 47</cite></blockquote>",
